<p dir="auto">A hosted, multi-user TradeX at <code>https://vtradingview.com/hub.html</code>: an Axum server replaces the Python
hub, Google sign-in saves each user's work, and a Tauri 2 app shows the same site on the desktop.</p>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto">Not affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to TradingView, Inc. "TradingView" is their trademark.
This project is an independent open-source tool. It is not financial advice.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Status: phases 1 to 3 complete on branch <code>arch/axum-tauri</code>; phase 4 (public deploy) in flight.</strong>
The Python hub is retired on this branch: the Rust server is the only server (see
<a href="#deploy">Deploy</a>). Remaining design-review findings are tracked as launch questions
(see <a href="#open-questions">Open Questions</a>).</p>
</blockquote>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Type:</strong> Application</li>
<li><strong>Stack:</strong> Rust (Axum 0.8, tokio, sqlx with the Postgres driver) + the existing vanilla JS frontend
(Vela, Lightweight Charts, KLineChart) + pgsqlite over a SQLite file by default, real Postgres by
<code>DATABASE_URL</code> + Tauri 2 desktop shell + mise tasks (dev) and systemd (prod) + Cloudflare Tunnel</li>
<li><strong>Skill Loadout:</strong> PAUL (required), AEGIS (post-build audit), <code>/seed design</code> (new screens),
security-review (auth, SSRF), postgres-pro (pgsqlite parity)</li>
<li><strong>Quality Gates:</strong> Rust-only contract suite, order-flow golden parity, CI on pgsqlite and
Postgres 17, cross-user authz tests, SSRF suite, design gates through
<code>.paul/runs/gate_all.py</code>, p75 time to first live candle under 2 s</li>
<li><strong>Design doc (single source):</strong> <a href="/baysao/open-source-tradingview/blob/ee3e1fe682888a5170500847e8113f9979563699/.paul/design/specs/vtradingview.md">.paul/design/specs/vtradingview.md</a></li>
<li><strong>Full plan:</strong> <a href="/baysao/open-source-tradingview/blob/ee3e1fe682888a5170500847e8113f9979563699/.paul/PLANNING.md">.paul/PLANNING.md</a></li>
</ul>
<div class="markdown-heading" dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" class="heading-element" dir="auto">Overview</h2><a id="user-content-overview" class="anchor" aria-label="Permalink: Overview" href="#overview"><svg data-component="Octicon" class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"></path></svg></a></div>
<p dir="auto">TradeX today needs Python 3, a virtual environment, numpy, pandas and pyarrow, and a terminal; whatever a
user builds lives in one browser's <code>localStorage</code>. This project makes it a public website, with charts
open to anyone and saved state (layouts, drawings, Pine scripts, favourites, indicators, theme) synced to
a Google account, plus a desktop app that shows the same site.</p>
<p dir="auto">The existing chart UI stays. Seven new screens are added in the hub's own theme: the sign-in control and
avatar menu, sync notices, MT5 bridges settings, delete account, the admin log panel, the desktop
sign-in waiting state, and a privacy page. Mockups: <code>.paul/design/opendesign/vtradingview/</code>.</p>
